// Car tuning. The server checks this file every second while it runs: edit, save, keep driving.
// An invalid file is reported in the server log and ignored. See docs/simulation.md.
(
    // Speeds in m/s (58 m/s = 209 km/h), accelerations in m/s². No brake: full speed all the time,
    // and letting go of the accelerator only coasts. The car is heavy: it takes its time getting up
    // to speed, and keeps what a dive or a boost gives it beyond top speed. The acceleration fades
    // out toward top speed, so top_speed / acceleration is how heavy it feels: 2.5 s, reaching 95%
    // of top speed in 7.5 s whatever the top speed. 40 and 16 until v0.1.34, 45 and 18 in v0.1.35.
    top_speed: 58.0,
    acceleration: 23.2,
    coasting: 3.0,
    // Per second: how quickly speed above top_speed fades, as a share of the excess. At 0.5 a dive
    // carries the car past top speed and a boost lingers; at 4 the excess is gone in about a
    // second; 0 keeps it for good.
    overspeed_drag: 0.5,

    // Turning radius at full lock, in meters: when slow, and at top speed. 32 at top speed until
    // v0.1.36; at 58 m/s that turned and slid so hard that half lock broke into a drift.
    turn_radius_slow: 6.0,
    turn_radius_fast: 38.0,
    // The car always steers at least as if it went this fast (m/s), so it can turn away from a wall
    // it is stuck against.
    min_steering_speed: 4.0,
    // Per second: how quickly the steering follows the stick. It moves on the very tick the stick
    // does and eases in as it gets there, so the car turns at once, gently, then a little harder;
    // at 14, most of the way in 0.15 s. Lower is softer, higher is sharper.
    steering_rate: 14.0,
    // Per second: how quickly the rotation follows the steering, well above steering_rate so it
    // adds no delay, and only smooths the change of radius when a drift starts or ends.
    steering_response: 60.0,
    // Degrees per second per second: the car's weight. Its rotation changes no faster than this,
    // gathering steadily from the very tick the steering moves and dying away the same way when the
    // stick lets go, so the car carries on round a little. Small corrections never meet it; a full
    // turn of the wheel and a slalom do. Higher is lighter: from about 1500 it holds nothing back.
    yaw_acceleration: 250.0,
    // Per second: how quickly sliding dies out while gripping. Lower lets the car's weight carry it
    // further to the outside of a turn before it follows its nose. Drifting is not affected: a
    // drift goes where the stick says. At 11.6 the travel trails the nose by 3.4 degrees at half
    // lock at top speed. A faster car slides further at the same grip, so it rose with the top
    // speed: 9 at 45 m/s (v0.1.32 to v0.1.35); at 6 (v0.1.14 to v0.1.31) the travel trailed 8.4
    // degrees at three quarters lock and 40 m/s, which carried the car out.
    grip: 11.6,
    // Pull down the slopes of the road, in m/s² (9.81 is Earth's): it slows a climb, speeds a dive
    // and draws the car to the inside of a banked turn. 0 makes the relief and the banking purely
    // visual.
    gravity: 9.81,

    // Drift, as in Rocket Racing: a tap of the drift button with the stick turned throws the car
    // into a drift, and so does turning hard enough to break away. The stick then sets the curve
    // the car travels on, the body swings far across it without carrying the car wide, the speed
    // holds and a charge builds up. Bringing the body back into line, stick centered or against
    // the drift, ends it and turns the charge into a boost.
    // Slowest speed a drift starts or keeps going at (m/s).
    drift_min_speed: 12.0,
    // How far off its axis of travel a gripping car must come to break into a drift without the
    // button, in degrees: at top speed full lock takes the slide past 5 degrees in 0.35 s and three
    // quarters lock in 0.52 s, half lock never does. It goes with grip: more grip, less slide, a
    // lower angle. At 7 (v0.1.32 and v0.1.33) only full lock broke away, after 0.52 s. 4.5 broke
    // away sooner still, and a client's autopilot six ticks late then scraped every wall.
    drift_entry_angle: 5.0,
    // How far back into line the body must come, with the stick keeping it there, for a drift to
    // end, in degrees. Holding the button keeps the drift alive whatever the body does.
    drift_exit_angle: 8.0,
    // How far the body swings across the travel with the steering fully into the drift, in
    // degrees, most of it with the first half of the steering. 55 (measured on Rocket Racing) threw
    // the car across too far for the user, and 42 (v0.1.32 and v0.1.33) still carried it across
    // too fast; 36 is the angle the car is seen sliding at.
    drift_angle: 36.0,
    // How the body swings there and back: it comes off the travel on the very tick the drift
    // starts, slowly, its swing gathering speed at this many degrees per second per second, up to
    // the fastest swing, in degrees per second, and slowing the same way to stop on its angle.
    // Past the knee, in degrees across, the body swings further out at no more than this share of
    // the fastest swing: the car is carried across less directly once it is well into its slide
    // (the user's word on 2026-11-12, v0.1.37). Swinging back into line is never held back.
    // With 150, 45, 15 and 0.5, a tap at full lock has the body 6 degrees out at 0.25 s, 17 at
    // 0.5 s, 23 at 0.75 s and 29 at 1 s, and a drift ends 0.8 s after the stick straightens.
    // Without the knee (v0.1.35) it was 6, 17, 28 and 36; at 260 and 75 (v0.1.32 and v0.1.33) 10,
    // 29 and 41 at 0.75 s, ending in 0.6 s; at 450 and 110 (v0.1.12 to v0.1.31) 15, 43 and 55.
    drift_swing_acceleration: 150.0,
    drift_swing_rate: 45.0,
    drift_swing_knee: 15.0,
    drift_swing_knee_rate: 0.5,
    // Radius of the curve the car drifts on with the stick fully to one side and the body swung
    // fully out, in meters: the curve tightens from the gripping car's to this one as the body
    // swings out. The stick is signed: centered runs straight and against the drift turns the
    // other way. The car goes where the stick says; it does not run wide. 26 until v0.1.36.
    drift_radius_tight: 30.0,
    // Per second: how quickly the curve follows the steering in a drift, as fast as the rotation
    // does while gripping, smoothing only the button's tightening.
    drift_steering_response: 60.0,
    // Holding the drift button multiplies the drift radius by this: it tightens the turn beyond
    // what the stick alone gives. Held with the stick well against the drift, it carries the drift
    // over to the other side, charge and all, through an S.
    drift_button_tighten: 0.7,
    // Seconds the button must stay held for that tightening to take full effect: it comes in
    // steadily from the press, so the tap that starts a drift barely tightens it.
    drift_button_tighten_time: 0.4,
    // What holding the button costs, in m/s² when it is pressed, then that much more per second it
    // stays held: a drift costs nothing, tightening it does, harder and harder.
    drift_button_drag: 1.5,
    drift_button_drag_ramp: 0.5,
    // Seconds a press of the drift button is remembered: pressed a moment before the stick is
    // turned, or before the car is fast enough, it still starts the drift. 0.15 until v0.1.34.
    drift_press_buffer: 0.25,
    // The hardest a drifting car can meet a wall and keep its drift, in m/s into the wall. A graze
    // is forgiven, and charges nothing while it lasts; a hit loses the drift and what it built up.
    drift_wall_tolerance: 3.0,
    // Charge before any boost (s), seconds of boost per second of charge beyond it, and the
    // longest boost (s). The charge grows from half to one and a half times real time, tighter drifts
    // faster, so a full charge takes two to three and a half seconds of drift.
    drift_min_charge: 0.3,
    drift_boost_rate: 0.5,
    drift_max_boost: 1.2,
    // Boost: top speed (m/s, 75 = 270 km/h, three tenths above top_speed) and acceleration (m/s²),
    // in full up to top_speed and fading out above it. After it, the car keeps its momentum: the
    // speed above top_speed fades at overspeed_drag.
    boost_top_speed: 75.0,
    boost_acceleration: 65.0,
    // Boost for a perfect start (s), pressing the accelerator right at the start. Grades from S to
    // E give from all of it to none.
    start_boost: 1.5,

    // Turbo: a meter of three charges that drifting fills, a charge spent at a press of the turbo
    // button on a boost stronger and longer than a drift's.
    // Charges filled per second of drift charge: at 0.2, a charge for every five seconds of
    // charge, which a tight drift builds in a little over three.
    turbo_fill: 0.2,
    // What a charge gives: seconds of boost, its top speed (m/s, 87 = 313 km/h, half as much again
    // as top_speed) and its acceleration (m/s²), fading out toward that top speed as a boost's
    // does.
    turbo_seconds: 2.0,
    turbo_top_speed: 87.0,
    turbo_acceleration: 80.0,

    // Walls. The bounce is the share of the impact speed that comes back, from 0 to 1. The impact
    // friction is the speed along the wall a hit takes, per m/s it takes into it: a graze costs
    // little, a blow at an angle a lot. The friction slows a car scraping along a wall, per second.
    collision_radius: 1.2,
    wall_bounce: 0.1,
    wall_impact_friction: 0.5,
    wall_friction: 2.5,
    // Per second: how quickly a wall turns a car that runs into it along itself, so the car comes
    // away from it instead of grinding on with its nose in. High, so it is done in a few ticks,
    // each of which is another hit; the body swings round after it in its own time. 0 leaves the
    // car pointing where it did.
    wall_alignment: 60.0,
)
